MVIEW_REWRITE: answering queries from materialized views
=======================================================

A post-parse query rewrite plugin.  A SELECT that can be computed from a
materialized view (CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW, see sql/mview.h) is rewritten
to read the view.  The view matching is the algorithm of

  J. Goldstein, P.-A. Larson: Optimizing Queries Using Materialized Views:
  A Practical, Scalable Solution.  SIGMOD 2001.

  INSTALL PLUGIN MVIEW_REWRITE SONAME 'mview_rewrite.so';

The server adds a note to a rewritten statement ("Query '...' rewritten to
'...' by a query rewrite plugin"), and EXPLAIN shows the plan of the
rewritten statement.


What is matched
---------------

A query block with inner joins (comma, JOIN ... ON), WHERE, GROUP BY,
aggregate functions, HAVING, DISTINCT, ORDER BY and LIMIT, against views
with inner joins (views with outer joins are not used):

- Column equivalence classes; the equijoin, range and residual subsumption
  tests; compensating equalities, ranges (numbers, ASCII strings in the
  collation of the column, dates and datetimes) and residual conditions
  (section 3.1).  Residual conditions match textually, with column
  references compared through the equivalence classes, and are computed
  from output expressions of the view when it has them.
- Tables of the view that the query does not have, when they are joined by
  NOT NULL foreign keys to unique keys (or the query rejects the NULLs):
  the foreign key join graph (section 3.2).  mview_rewrite_use_foreign_keys
  turns this off (foreign keys may be violated with foreign_key_checks=0).
- Aggregate views (section 3.3): the same groups (the stored states are
  read), or coarser groups (COUNT, SUM, MIN, MAX and AVG are rolled up from
  the states; MIN, MAX, BIT_AND, BIT_OR and DISTINCT aggregates of group
  expressions are computed over the groups), and SELECT DISTINCT of group
  expressions.  Conditions on an aggregate view must be on its group
  expressions.
- A view may replace some of the tables of the query; the other tables are
  joined to it (the view-matching rule applied to a subexpression).
  mview_rewrite_partial turns this off.
- The filter tree of section 4: lattice indexes over the hubs of the
  views, their residual conditions and, for aggregate views, their tables.
  mview_rewrite_use_filter_tree=OFF tests every view (the rewrite is the
  same).

The optimizer of MySQL cannot compare the costs of rewrites at parse
time; among the views that match the plugin takes the one that replaces
the most tables, then one that aggregates, then one with the same groups,
then fewer extra tables and fewer compensating conditions.  A join view
that would replace one table without filtering it is not used.


Which views answer a statement
------------------------------

- REFRESH SYNCHRONOUS views while the WARP_MVIEW daemon maintains them
  (not stale, the definition it maintains is the current one).  They trail
  the commits of their base tables by the delay of the daemon; a session
  that committed a change does not use them before the daemon has applied
  that change (read your writes, from the binary log positions).
- REFRESH MANUAL views only with mview_rewrite_use_manual_views=ON: they
  hold the data of their last refresh.
- The user needs SELECT on the base tables (as for the original statement)
  and on the view.


Common table expressions
------------------------

In a statement with a WITH clause, the query expression of every common
table expression that is one query block is rewritten as if it were a
statement of its own (the rules above), and replaces the original in the
text of the statement.  The rest of the statement is not changed and may be
anything: joins of the common table expressions, subqueries, set
operations, outer joins, window functions, ROLLUP.

  WITH t AS (SELECT k, SUM(v) AS s FROM f GROUP BY k)
  SELECT ... FROM t t1 WHERE t1.s > (SELECT AVG(s) FROM t t2 ...)
->
  WITH t AS (SELECT `mv`.`mv$g0` AS `k`, `mv`.`mv$s1` AS `s` FROM `d`.`mv` `mv`)
  SELECT ... FROM t t1 WHERE t1.s > (SELECT AVG(s) FROM t t2 ...)

Only the WITH clause of the statement itself is looked at (not one in a
subquery or derived table), and only common table expressions that the
statement reads.  A recursive one, one with set operations or with a WITH
clause of its own is left as it is; so is a query block that reads another
common table expression (a name that is a common table expression is not
the base table of that name).  With mview_rewrite_trace the notes name the
common table expression.


Statements that are not rewritten
---------------------------------

Prepared statements and statements of stored programs; subqueries,
derived tables, set operations, outer joins, NATURAL and USING joins,
window functions, ROLLUP (all of these outside of a common table
expression that is rewritten); locking reads, SELECT ... INTO, optimizer hints,
SQL_CALC_FOUND_ROWS; tables with partition selection or TABLESAMPLE,
temporary tables; statements in a transaction that has changed data, under
LOCK TABLES, of replication threads, of users with SKIP_QUERY_REWRITE;
sessions with ANSI_QUOTES or NO_BACKSLASH_ESCAPES, a character_set_client
other than the character set of collation_connection, or another
collation_connection than the default of the server.

A string column of a WARP view stores a binary collation: it does not
stand for a column of another collation.


Variables
---------

  mview_rewrite_enabled             ON   rewrite statements
  mview_rewrite_use_manual_views    OFF  use REFRESH MANUAL views
  mview_rewrite_use_foreign_keys    ON   eliminate extra tables of views
  mview_rewrite_partial             ON   views for some of the tables
  mview_rewrite_use_filter_tree     ON   search candidates with the tree
  mview_rewrite_trace               OFF  notes on why views are not used

  Status: Mview_rewrite_statements, Mview_rewrite_rewritten,
  Mview_rewrite_candidates (views tested in full),
  Mview_rewrite_registry_loads, Mview_rewrite_views.

The definitions of the views are loaded by a thread of the plugin when the
materialized views change; while that load runs, a statement that holds
metadata locks is not rewritten instead of waiting.


Files
-----

  mview_rewrite.h            the algorithm and the data structures
  mview_rewrite_plugin.cc    the plugin, the registry and its loader
  mv_catalog.cc              table definitions from the data dictionary
  mv_expr.cc                 expressions and query blocks
  mv_match.cc                view matching, the rewritten statement
  mv_filter.cc               lattice index and filter tree

Tests: mysql-test/suite/warp/t/mview_rewrite.test, mview_rewrite_cte.test,
mview_rewrite_sync.test.
